# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification" (HoareLogic Exercises.pdf), problem 5 a), with its solution sheet.
id: transfer-y-to-x
title: Moving the value of y into x
topic: Loops
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 5 a
tags: while, invariant, logical variables

task:
  Give a proof outline and a loop invariant. The variables $x'$ and $y'$
  are logical variables: they keep the initial values of $x$ and $y$.

pre: x = x' && y = y' && y >= 0
post: x = x' + y'

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (y > 0) do {
    {I && y > 0}
    {R2}
    x := x + 1;      // b1
    {R1}
    y := y - 1       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(y > 0)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  Each iteration adds one to $x$ and removes one from $y$, so the sum
  $x + y$ does not change. At the start it is $x' + y'$. The variable $y$
  goes down from $y'$ to $0$, which gives the bounds of $y$.
set: I = 0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y'
text:
  The loop rule with consequence needs three checks, with the loop
  condition $B$ being $y > 0$: establishment $P ==> I$, preservation of
  $I$ by the body, and exit $I && !B ==> Q$.

step: Establishment: P implies I
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  No statement comes before the loop, so we prove that the precondition
  $P$ implies $I$.
  >>> «x = x' && y = y' && y >= 0 : P» ==> «0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y' : I»
vc: x = x' && y = y' && y >= 0 ==> 0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y'
why:
  $0 <= y$ is part of $P$, and $y = y'$ gives $y <= y'$. Replacing $x$ by
  $x'$ and $y$ by $y'$ turns $x + y = x' + y'$ into $x' + y' = x' + y'$,
  which is true.

step: Preservation: R1, the weakest precondition of y := y - 1 for I
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  We prove that one iteration keeps the invariant:
  >>> {«0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y' : I» && «y > 0 : B»} x := x + 1; y := y - 1 {«0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y' : I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $y$ with $y - 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(y := y - 1, I)
  = (0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y')[y <- y - 1]
  = 0 <= y - 1 <= y' && x + (y - 1) = x' + y'     // y is replaced by y - 1
  = 1 <= y <= y' + 1 && x + y - 1 = x' + y'       // add 1 to each part of the bounds
set: R1 = 1 <= y <= y' + 1 && x + y - 1 = x' + y'

step: Preservation: R2, the weakest precondition of x := x + 1 for R1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $x$ with $x + 1$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, R1)
  = (1 <= y <= y' + 1 && x + y - 1 = x' + y')[x <- x + 1]
  = 1 <= y <= y' + 1 && (x + 1) + y - 1 = x' + y'    // x is replaced by x + 1
  = 1 <= y <= y' + 1 && x + y = x' + y'              // + 1 and - 1 cancel
set: R2 = 1 <= y <= y' + 1 && x + y = x' + y'

step: Preservation: I and B imply R2
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y' : I» && «y > 0 : B» ==> «1 <= y <= y' + 1 && x + y = x' + y' : R2»
vc: 0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y' && y > 0 ==> 1 <= y <= y' + 1 && x + y = x' + y'
why:
  For integers, $y > 0$ is $1 <= y$. From $y <= y'$ we get $y <= y' + 1$.
  The equation $x + y = x' + y'$ is part of $I$.

step: Exit: I and not B imply Q
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y' : I» && «!(y > 0) : ¬B» ==> «x = x' + y' : Q»
vc: 0 <= y <= y' && x + y = x' + y' && !(y > 0) ==> x = x' + y'
why:
  $!(y > 0)$ is $y <= 0$. Together with $0 <= y$ it gives $y = 0$, so
  $x + y = x' + y'$ becomes $x = x' + y'$.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The loop also terminates: $y$ decreases by one in every iteration, and it
  is positive whenever the body runs, so $y$ is a variant.
